.TH BIFIN 1 "October 2026" "BIDeT 4D" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bifin \- make a BIF (BIDeT Intermediate Format) from terminal art, text in a font, or a recorded terminal session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bifin
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIFILE\fR]
.br
.I command
|
.B bifin
[\fIoptions\fR] |
.B bifop
\&... |
.B bifout
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bifin
is the first tool of BIDeT 4D.  It reads terminal art (ASCII, ANSI, Unicode, DOS art in CP437), a string to be set
in a font, or a recorded terminal session, and writes a BIF: vectors and rasters, a palette of colours with roles, the
character grid, and, for a session, one frame for each screen.  The BIF goes to standard output or to
.BR \-o ;
it is meant for
.BR bifop (1)
and
.BR bifout (1).
.B bifin
will not write a BIF to a terminal.
.PP
With no
.I FILE
it reads standard input.  Unlike a picture of the art, the BIF keeps what the art was made of: strokes stay sharp at any
size, letters are outlines read from a font, and the colours are a palette that can be changed afterwards.
.SH INPUT KINDS
The kind is chosen with
.BR \-f ,
or, if it is not given, from the file name (a name ending in
.I .vt
is a session) and then from what the input looks like.
.TP
.B text
(the default for art) Terminal art, UTF-8 or CP437, with ANSI colour, cursor addressing and scroll regions replayed on a
terminal emulator, so that screen dumps work.  A SAUCE record is read for its title, credit and width.  Name the mode with
.BR \-m .
.TP
.B lettering
A string set in a proportional font: the font's own advance widths and kerning, one filled vector layer, sharp at any size.
The text is the input, or the file.
.TP
.B vt
A terminal session, as
.B slowcat
plays one: it is replayed, the screen is taken at every newline (before the newline is processed), and each distinct screen is
a frame.  All the frames are drawn alike, and the canvas is cut to what any of them draws.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS General
.TP
.BI \-o " FILE" "\fR, " \-\-output " FILE"
Write the BIF here (default: standard output).
.TP
.BR \-f " \fIKIND\fR, " \-\-from " \fIKIND"
.BR text ,
.B lettering
or
.BR vt .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Say what was decided (the mode, the font, the strokes).
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SS Art
.TP
.BR \-m " \fIMODE\fR, " \-\-mode " \fIMODE"
.B lineart
redraws line art (cowsay, figlet) as a line drawing, and outlines picture-style art;
.BR ansi\-block " (or " block )
draws art made of blocks and graphic characters as the coloured picture it is;
.B auto
(the default) chooses, and when the art is of several kinds at once (a message of the day: a logo, a figlet title, a
picture of half blocks, lines of plain text) it cuts the art into regions and draws each the way that suits it
(see MIXED ART).
.B mixed
asks for exactly that.
.B text
sets every character in the font, as a line of prose.
.BR line ", " tone " and " mix
force one method of
.BR lineart :
joined strokes; traced outlines and contour lines of picture-style art (jp2a, chafa); both.
.TP
.BI \-c " PX" "\fR, " \-\-cell " PX"
The nominal width of one character cell in the file's units (default 12).
.TP
.BI \-a " ASPECT" "\fR, " \-\-aspect " ASPECT"
Cell height divided by width (default 2.0).
.TP
.BI \-w " WEIGHT" "\fR, " \-\-weight " WEIGHT"
The thickness of the pen (default 1).
.TP
.BI \-\-cols " N" "\fR, " \-\-rows " N"
The width at which art wraps (default: from SAUCE, else 80 for a
.I .ans
file, else never), and the height of the screen that cursor addressing and scroll regions refer to (default 24).  For a
session
.B \-\-cols
is the terminal's width (default 80).
.TP
.BI \-\-encoding " NAME"
The input's encoding (default: UTF-8, else CP437).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-crop
Keep the margin round the drawing.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-cells
Do not keep the character grid in the file (a hidden
.B cells
layer).
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-source
Store the input itself in the file.
.SS Colour
.TP
.BR \-\-color " \fIauto\fR|\fIon\fR|\fIoff\fR (or " \-\-colour "), " \-\-mono
Keep the ANSI colours: if the art has any (default), always, or never.
.B \-\-mono
is one colour, the palette's ink.
.TP
.BI \-\-ink " COLOR" "\fR, " \-\-paper " COLOR"
The ink and the page colour in the palette (default black on white; light grey on black for colour block art).
.TP
.BR \-\-dark ", " \-\-invert
Tone mode: the art is light on dark (on by itself when the art uses colour); outline the negative.
.SS Line mode
.TP
.BI \-j " CELLS" "\fR, " \-\-join " CELLS"
Join stroke ends this close, in cell widths (default 1; 0 = never).
.TP
.BI \-\-spline " F" "\fR, " \-\-no\-round
Spline smoothing of joined strokes, in cell widths (default 0.6; 0 = only round the corners); or no smoothing at all.
.TP
.BI \-\-shade " CHARS" "\fR, " \-\-hatch " F"
Runs of these characters are shading and get hatched (default X; the empty string = none); the spacing of the hatching.
.TP
.BI \-\-text\-bold " F"
How much letters thicken with a weight above 1.2 (default 0.25; 0 = never).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-outlines
Letters as a raster layer, not vector outlines.
.SS Tone mode
.TP
.BI \-\-smooth " F" "\fR, " \-\-detail " F" "\fR, " \-\-scale " F" "\fR, " \-\-levels " N"
Blur in dot pitches (default 0.7; for block art, the softening of the pixels, default 0.12, 0 = crisp); the smallest tonal
step that gets an outline (0 to 1, default 0.12); the finest outline feature in dot pitches (default 0.4); the contour lines
through the shading (default 3, 0 = outlines only).
.TP
.BI \-\-tone\-fill " WHEN"
Tone art that is one solid shape is drawn as filled vector shapes, one colour for each band of colour, with
holes where the art has gaps, and no outline (the shape is the outline).  A shape is solid when most of its characters
are one character (a logo of
.B @@@@
or
.BR MMMM ),
or when every character has a colour of its own choosing and there are at most 8 colours (a wordmark built from every
letter of the alphabet, as in a CloudLinux logo).  A picture made of shading, whose tone comes from the terminal's
default colour, is not solid.  A mode forced with
.B \-m tone
or
.B \-m mix
is never filled automatically: it asks for tone's outlines.
.I WHEN
is
.B auto
(the default: art that is solid, in mixed art or alone),
.B on
(every tone picture: only sensible for solid shapes) or
.BR off
(outlines only).  The layer is called
.BR fill .
.TP
.B \-\-tone\-raster
The outlines as a raster layer, not traced vector lines.
.SS Fonts
.TP
.BI \-\-font " FONT"
Art: the monospace TrueType font, a file or a name.  With
.B \-f lettering
the same as
.BR \-\-face .
.TP
.BI \-\-face " FACE"
Lettering: the font to set the text in: a file, a family (\fBimpact\fR, \fB"Times New Roman"\fR), a generic name
(\fBsans\fR, \fBserif\fR, \fBmono\fR, \fBscript\fR, \fBcomic\fR ...) or several joined with commas (default \fBsans\fR).
Art ignores it, so that a recipe can ask for a face without breaking the art it is used on.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-fonts
List the font families found, and exit.
.SS Lettering (\-f lettering)
.TP
.BI \-\-size " PX" "\fR, " \-\-spacing " EM" "\fR, " \-\-line\-height " EM" "\fR, " \-\-margin " EM"
Font size in the units of the picture (default 100); extra space between letters in ems (default 0); the distance between
lines (default: the font's); the space round the letters (default 0.15).
.TP
.BI \-\-align " left" | center | right "\fR, " \-\-wrap " PX"
Alignment of several lines (default left); break lines at spaces to fit this width (default: never).
.TP
.B \-\-one\-line
Set it all on one line: every run of white space (a line break, an indent) becomes one space.  bidet does this for
text that scrolls sideways.
.TP
.BR \-\-bold ", " \-\-italic
A bold or italic face; one the family lacks is faked (an outline; a slant).
.SS Terminal sessions (\-f vt, or a file named *.vt)
.TP
.BI \-\-delay " MS" "\fR, " \-\-hold " MS"
How long each newline lasts, as
.BR slowcat 's
argument (default 100); how long the last screen stays (default 1000).
.TP
.BI \-\-fps " N" "\fR, " \-\-loop " N"
At most this many frames a second (default: a frame for every change); how many times it plays, 0 = forever (default).
.SH MIXED ART
A screen of
.BR neofetch ,
.BR linux_logo
or a message of the day is rarely one kind of art: a logo drawn with strokes, a title in figlet or toilet, a picture
of half blocks or shaded characters, and the lines of plain text beside them.  One method for the whole grid
blurs the text with the picture or traces it as outlines.  So with
.B \-m auto
(or
.BR "\-m mixed" )
.B bifin
looks at the cells first.  Lines of words (two words, or a word and a number, with the numbers and punctuation
between them; not shading made of letters, not a cowsay bubble) are
.BR text :
every character is a letter from the font, as sharp outlines.  Cells that are pixels (half blocks, braille, shades,
a colour behind them) are a
.BR block
picture.  The rest is cut into pieces of art that lie close together (a piece that comes out as dense fill with strokes
among it is split into its fill and its stroke pieces), and each piece gets
.BR line ", " tone " or " mix
as if it stood alone.  The layers are put together on one canvas with one palette (the layers keep their usual ids;
a second layer of the same kind gets the method as a suffix: strokes, strokes\-mix); the page is black when there
is a coloured picture or the screen says black behind every cell, as for colour block art; the picture is cut to
what is drawn.
.B bifin \-v
prints the share of each method, and
.B python bifin_regions.py FILE
prints the map (T text, B block, L line, N tone, X mix).
.PP
Art that is one method, or line art with its words, comes out as before (the words are set in the font even where the
characters would pass for drawing: the 8 of a kernel version, a #).  A file is only ever cut when the pieces need
different methods.  The cutting is a judgement of what looks like words: if it is wrong for a file, force a method with
.BR "\-m line" ", " "\-m tone " "or " "\-m ansi\-block" .
Animations (\-f vt) are not cut: their mode is chosen once from the fullest screen.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cowsay hi | bifin | bifout \-s            a cow, on the terminal
bifin motd | bifout \-o motd.png          a message of the day: logo, title, picture and text each in its own way
bifin art.ans \-o art.bif                 DOS art, kept as a BIF
bifin \-m ansi\-block art.ans | bifout \-o art.png
echo Hello | bifin \-f lettering \-\-face impact \-\-size 120 | bifout \-s
bifin bambi.vt \-\-delay 25 \-\-hold 3000 | bifout \-o bambi.gif
.fi
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B bifin
exits with a message on standard error and a non-zero status if the input cannot be read, or if no importer fits it.
A row of only dashes, or a lone
.BR x ,
is read as tone art and draws nothing in line mode (this is how the original tool read it, kept on purpose).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bifop (1),
.BR bifout (1),
.BR bidet (1).
The format is described in BIF\-SPEC.md, and
.B bif.py info FILE
says what a BIF holds.
